# KubeVerse desktop shell - environment configuration template.
# Copy the variable(s) you need into your real shell/CI environment before
# launching KubeVerse. This file is never read automatically - it exists
# only as safe, credential-free documentation of what can be configured.
# Never commit a real value here.

# Required for "Continue with Google" to be available at all. Without it,
# the sign-in button is simply not offered - KubeVerse still launches and
# works fully without it (Google identity is optional; see
# KUBEVERSE_MASTER_SPEC.md §7).
#
# How to get a real value:
#   1. Create (or reuse) a project in https://console.cloud.google.com/
#   2. Configure the OAuth consent screen (External or Internal, as fits
#      your use case).
#   3. Create an OAuth 2.0 Client ID with application type "Desktop app".
#   4. Copy the Client ID (NOT the client secret - KubeVerse never uses one,
#      by design; see googleAuth.js's own comments for why a Desktop app
#      OAuth client doesn't need it here).
#
# This value is not a secret - it identifies your OAuth client, not a
# credential that grants access on its own - but it is still specific to
# your own Google Cloud project, so no real value is provided here.
KUBEVERSE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# Required, alongside KUBEVERSE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID above, for "Continue with
# Google" to be available (Phase 6: Firebase Authentication brokers the
# actual sign-in - see KUBEVERSE_MASTER_SPEC.md §7 and desktop/src/
# firebaseAuth.js). Missing either one disables sign-in gracefully; nothing
# else in KubeVerse depends on it.
#
# How to get a real value:
#   1. Create (or reuse) a project at https://console.firebase.google.com/
#      (a Firebase project IS a Google Cloud project - the same one from
#      KUBEVERSE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID's own setup steps can be reused).
#   2. Authentication -> Sign-in method -> enable "Google" as a provider.
#   3. Still on that Google provider's settings, open "Whitelist client IDs
#      from external projects" and add the exact Desktop app OAuth Client ID
#      you set as KUBEVERSE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID above - without this step,
#      Firebase rejects the Google ID token KubeVerse presents to it with an
#      audience-mismatch error, even though the client ID itself is valid.
#   4. Project settings -> General -> "Your apps" -> add a Web app (any
#      nickname) if one doesn't already exist, then copy its "Web API Key".
#
# This is a Firebase "Web API key", not an Admin SDK service-account key -
# Firebase's own security guidance states API keys restricted to Firebase
# services "do not need to be treated as secrets, and it's safe to include
# them in your code or configuration files" (unlike an Admin SDK private
# key, which KubeVerse never uses and must never be shipped). It is still
# specific to your own Firebase project, so no real value is provided here.
KUBEVERSE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
